  1	# Release acceptance
  2	
  3	From the repository root, run the checker tests and record the four Vitest configurations, including results for all three browser engines.
  4	
  5	```sh
  6	node --test acceptance/test/*.test.mjs
  7	node acceptance/run.mjs unit
  8	node acceptance/run.mjs browser
  9	AXYLO_ENGINE=firefox node acceptance/run.mjs browser
 10	AXYLO_ENGINE=webkit node acceptance/run.mjs browser
 11	node acceptance/run.mjs dist
 12	node acceptance/run.mjs dist-browser
 13	node acceptance/check.mjs --release --write --results \
 14	  acceptance/results/unit.json acceptance/results/browser.json \
 15	  acceptance/results/browser-firefox.json acceptance/results/browser-webkit.json \
 16	  acceptance/results/dist.json acceptance/results/dist-browser.json
 17	```
 18	
 19	Build the packages before the browser and distribution configurations. Each wrapper run writes Vitest JSON and a neighbouring `*.provenance.json` with the Git commit, dirty flag, tree fingerprint, result hash and hashes of every cited test file. The sidecar records a working-tree change during Vitest and the release checker rejects that run. The generated `acceptance/report.md` does not affect the fingerprint. Supply the result paths to the checker and keep their sidecars beside them. For a filtered run, use `--output` to keep the full configuration’s result intact.
 20	
 21	The checker prints provenance, the matrix and the sources of each result. It writes [report.md](report.md) only with `--write`. Without results, it checks citations and shows static coverage and any cited packed size measurements. An omitted result leaves its assertions unverified. By default the checker can display historical reports, including archived absolute paths that carry a full repository-relative suffix.
 22	
 23	Use `--roots <directory>…` for result paths relative to additional roots such as `tests/dist` or `packages/react`. Paths that match more than one cited file are structural errors.
 24	
 25	Structural errors exit 1. In `--release` mode, results must have provenance that matches the working tree.
 26	
 27	## Release gate and statuses
 28	
 29	**Owner’s decision, 2026-11-19, “Documented gaps”.** Each cell must pass, be `not applicable`, qualify as weak with a documented gap, or carry the owner’s waiver for `not run` or `cannot be verified here`. A weak cell qualifies only if its `gaps` entry names what remains unverified and every cited result passes for this revision. The checker verifies that cited documents exist. The report lists qualifying weak cells under “Released with documented gaps” and gives each gap in full.
 30	
 31	A failure, stale result, pending measurement, or weak cell missing the gap or a passing result blocks release. The owner can explicitly waive a `not run` or `cannot be verified here` cell by adding `"waivers": { "<chart type>": { "reason": "…", "grantedBy": "…" } }` to its criterion. Pending measurements always block. The waiver appears in the report and leaves the original status visible.
 32	
 33	| Status | Meaning |
 34	|---|---|
 35	| pass | All cited assertions passed and measured targets were met. |
 36	| fail | A cited assertion or suite failed, or a measured target was exceeded. |
 37	| weak | Partial evidence leaves a gap. Release requires a non-empty `gaps` entry and current passing results for all result citations. |
 38	| no evidence | The criterion applies and has no relevant evidence. |
 39	| not run | A cited assertion is missing or skipped, or the cell awaits a valid measurement. |
 40	| not recorded for this revision | Its only answers come from runs or a size record whose provenance is another tree or pack. |
 41	| cannot be verified here | The criterion requires an environment or rehearsal outside the supplied runs. |
 42	| not applicable | The matrix records why the criterion does not apply. |
 43	
 44	## Browser engines
 45	
 46	The browser configuration runs on the engine `AXYLO_ENGINE` names: `chromium` (system Chrome, the default), `firefox` or `webkit` (Playwright's own builds; WebKit is the engine, not Safari). Each engine gets its own result file:
 47	
 48	```sh
 49	node acceptance/run.mjs browser                        # acceptance/results/browser.json
 50	AXYLO_ENGINE=firefox node acceptance/run.mjs browser   # acceptance/results/browser-firefox.json
 51	AXYLO_ENGINE=webkit node acceptance/run.mjs browser    # acceptance/results/browser-webkit.json
 52	```
 53	
 54	The harness records `meta.engine` and `meta.engineVersion` on every test, and the sidecar records the engine asked for and the version the results declare. The checker refuses a result file that mixes engines or disagrees with its sidecar. A browser citation takes an optional `engine` (default `chromium`) and is answered only by results of that engine; a pass on one engine never stands for another. A test that reported itself not run on an engine (`meta.notRun`) is `not run` with that reason. Results without engine records predate the switch and count as Chrome; in a run on another engine, a test without a record (the docs and distribution suites launch Chrome themselves) counts for no engine.
 55	
 56	## Reading the checker from code
 57	
 58	`check()` (acceptance/check.mjs) returns the Markdown `report` and `table`, and the same content as data: `rows` (per criterion its `id`, `statement` and, per chart type, `status`, `releasedGap`, `reasons` and `reports`, each with `file`, `label`, `source`, `results`, `engine`, `engineVersion` and `provenanceError`) and `runs`. `releasedGap` is the gap text for a weak cell that qualifies under the release gate in release mode, and `null` otherwise. `STATUSES` and `CHART_TYPES` are exported beside `check()`. acceptance/provenance.mjs exports the configurations (`CONFIGS`, `CONFIGURATIONS`), `ENGINES`, `engineLabel()`, `defaultResultFile()` and `resultFiles()`, the default result file of every configuration and engine.
 59	
 60	## Review and cite evidence
 61	
 62	Use the spec ID and short wording. Section 3.6 cells use `T-<rule>-<chart type>`. Give every excluded chart type a `notApplicable` reason. Compact covers line, area and bar variants where the rule applies.
 63	
 64	Use `evidence` for the checks a cell needs, `gaps` for the unverified part when those checks are incomplete, and `weak` for supporting citations with a specific `reason`. Use `limits` to describe the scope of a result, `cannotVerify` for environmental requirements outside the supplied runs, and `pending` for work awaiting a valid run.
 65	
 66	Each citation specifies a repository-relative `file` and a `kind` of `unit`, `property`, `browser`, `size`, `benchmark`, `document` or `manual rehearsal`. A test citation gives its exact Vitest `title` or a `prefix` of a describe title when all reported cases are required. A generated title may include its source template as `registration`. Use `ancestors` to cite a specific suite when titles repeat. The checker refuses citations of `.fails`, `.todo` and `.skip` and requires every matching result to pass.
 67	
 68	A suite whose cases the source scan cannot list (an `it.each` over rows computed at run time, such as DX10's per-example renders) is cited by its describe `prefix` with `atLeast`: the citation passes when every reported test under the prefix passed, none was skipped, the titles the scan does know are among them, and at least that many distinct tests passed. The `kind` must fit the file: `browser` for `*.browser.test.*` files, `unit` and `property` for the others. A title the scan finds only through a template, or only under an `if`, `else`, `&&`, `||` or `?:`, needs a run that shows it: a supplied result of its file or the `EVIDENCE.json` beside it. Without either the report lists a warning; a result or `EVIDENCE.json` that covers the file but lacks the title is a structural error. A `size` citation counts only when the record names the tarball integrity of the pack this tree makes (`npm pack`, as `scripts/pack.mjs` packs); the targets are `SIZE_TARGETS` in `scripts/size.mjs`. A run whose provenance sidecar does not match the tree, in any mode, and in a release a run without a sidecar, leaves its cells `not recorded for this revision`.
 69	
 70	For a describe suite shared across chart types, add a chart-specific `casePrefix` and the expected number of distinct `cases`. The checker requires all statically known cases and the expected number of passing current registrations. A failed archived result within that scope still appears in the report. Release checks also require matching provenance.
 71	
 72	A `size` citation names the budgeted `entries` in the packed size artifact. Historical benchmarks carry a `workload` and optional `history`. Documents, manual rehearsals and provisional benchmark workloads need separate review.
 73	
 74	The archived four-configuration review includes [review notes](review.json) with the prior status baseline, failure causes and the browser helpers checked by negative controls.

